erships with the entertainment industry to promote authentic LGBTQ stories and representation."}], "places": [{"name": "New York City, USA", "role": "Headquarters"}, {"name": "Los Angeles, USA", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "Washington, D.C., USA", "role": "Advocacy Center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: GLAAD", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "glaad"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1320972", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GLAAD"], "tags": ["United States", "nonprofit organization", "Americas", "Humanitarian & Relief Organizations", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "GLAAD contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "GLAAD played a crucial role in shifting societal perceptions of LGBTQ individuals, catalyzing legislative changes that led to greater rights and acceptance. Its media advocacy has helped redefine cultural narratives, contributing to a significant increase in public support for LGBTQ issues over the decades.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-29T20:51:51Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30489932949", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Us-based lgbtq media monitoring group Headquartered in New York City. Founded 1985.", "newValue": "GLAAD, or the Gay & Lesbian Alliance Against Defamation, emerged in 1985 in New York City as a response to the pervasive discrimination and negative media portrayal of LGBTQ individuals. Founded by a group of activists including Joan Garry and David Gehner, GLAAD sought to promote fair representation and advocacy for LGBTQ rights within the media landscape, marking a pivotal moment in the struggle for equality. During a time when the AIDS crisis was ravaging the community, GLAAD's mission became increasingly urgent, reflecting the broader societal shifts towards acceptance and understanding of LGBTQ issues across North America.

In its formative years, GLAAD launched several campaigns aimed at combating misinformation and stereotypes perpetuated by the media. One of its significant initiatives was the "Media Awards," instituted in 1990 to recognize and honor media representations of LGBTQ individuals, which helped to raise awareness and foster a more inclusive narrative. The organization also played a crucial role in various high-profile advocacy campaigns, including the fight against discriminatory legislation and the push for marriage equality, culminating in landmark achievements such as the legalization of same-sex marriage in many states by the early 2010s.

